Booleans and offsets: the intersection of an edge with a face
(IntTools_BeanFaceIntersector::ComputeAroundExactIntersection) asks
IntCurveSurface_HInter for the points where the edge's curve meets the
face's surface, and for a surface that is neither a quadric nor a plane
HInter samples each piece of the surface into a polyhedron (up to 40 x 40
points with their derivatives, and its deflection) for every curve. A
boolean's or an offset's faces meet many edges, so each face's polyhedra
were made again for every edge (mitcad#1823: the closed offsets of lofts
spent a third of their time making them). HInter gets Perform(Curve,
Surface, Polyhedra), the same intersection as Perform(Curve, Surface) with
the polyhedron of each piece taken from Polyhedra where made before for
the same bounds and kept there where made now, and IntTools_Context keeps
the polyhedra of each face for its edge/face intersections: the same
polyhedra, so the same intersection points. The kept polyhedra are listed
in an NCollection_List (OCCT's containers, mitcad#1986).

Test: geometry/tests/test_kernel_repeated_work.cpp
(geometry.kernel_repeated_work: the intersections with the kept polyhedra
are those made without them). The speed itself: none, because the gain (a
third of a closed offset's time) is below what a bound in a test of
seconds can tell on a loaded machine.
